  verification = {verified at listed scope},
  note = {Verified in this writing on 18 November 2026 at these loci only. Original journal text opened: Proposition1.13 pp.233-234; section2.1 p.234; Theorem2.18 pp.237-238; section2.19 and Proposition2.20 pp.238-239; Theorem2.21 p.239. Scope: Opened the reproduced original journal text and exact analytic hypotheses, local monomial Koszul calculation, canonical base change, residue spectrum and exponential-monodromy formula. The text of section2 does not assume Kahlerness or projectivity. This opening used the journal-text transcription; the primary PDF download remains unavailable. Original Invent.Math.31(1976) journal text reproduction: §1.4–1.6 pp231–232; Proposition1.13 full monomial Koszul proof pp233–234; §2.1 p234; lemmas2.4–2.15 and Proposition2.16 pp234–237; Theorem2.18 pp237–238; §§2.19–2.21 pp238–239. Scope: Actually opened and read the reproduced original text of the full analytic comparison chain. Section2 assumes a proper holomorphic map from a connected complex manifold to a disc, smooth off zero, with normal-crossing fibre support and arbitrary positive multiplicities; there is no Kahler or projectivity premise. The local diagonal Koszul operators yield the exact logarithmic cohomology, including gcd-of-multiplicities components. Theorem2.18 gives local freeness and canonical fibre base change; §2.19 gives the Gauss–Manin connection, Proposition2.20 residue eigenvalues in[0,1), and Theorem2.21 exp(−2pi i Res)=monodromy. Identity monodromy makes the residue zero, including its nilpotent part. The comparison in Proposition2.16 depends on the local base parameter as explicitly warned in2.17; the receiving global map instead uses the canonical log-complex projection and canonical extension. This source opening uses a journal-text reproduction; no available original PDF was independently inspected.}
